><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Schlessel</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Mestrandrea</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Rodgers</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Wapnir</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Neonatal outcome of infants born at 500 to 800 grams from 1990 through 1998 in a tertiary care center</article-title><source>J Perinatol</source><year>2002</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>555</fpage><lpage>562</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12368972</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.jp.7210789</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hoekstra</surname><given-names>RE</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferrara</surname><given-names>TB</given-names></name><name><surname>Couser</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Payne</surname><given-names>NR</given-names></name><name><surname>Connett</surname><given-names>JE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Survival and long-term neurodevelopmental outcome of extremely premature infants born at 23-26 weeks' gestational age at a tertiary center</article-title><source>Pediatrics</source><year>2004</year><volume>113</volume><fpage>e1</fpage><lpage>e6</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14702487</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1542/peds.113.1.e1</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Goldstein</surname><given-names>DJ</given-names></name><name><surname>EE</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>JL</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>TM</surname><given-names>OS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Comparison of the Bayley scales of infant development 2nd ed.Bayley scales of infant development with premature infants</art